---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";

// Charity not chosen yet. Set these once the family decides.
const CHARITY = { name: "", url: "" };
// The photo-essay version of this page (full-bleed photos, scroll scenes) is kept in src/drafts/photo-essay.astro.
---
<Base title="Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, 1929 to 2026">
  <Nav current="obituary" />

  <header class="cover plain">
    <div class="cover-title col">
      <span class="lady" data-reveal>Lady</span>
      <h1 data-split>Margaret</h1>
      <span class="fullname" data-reveal>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · 1929 – 2026</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="col prose">
      <p class="first">Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, who left wartime Glasgow at nineteen with little to her name, crossed the Atlantic alone, and remade herself in Montreal as a model, a businesswoman, and, in the eyes of her family, a lady, died peacefully on November [X], 2026, in Montreal, surrounded by family. She was 97.</p>
      <p>Hers was a life that spanned nearly a century, crossed an ocean, and held more reinventions, heartbreaks, and triumphs than most people could imagine. Some people pass through life quietly. Margaret was not one of them.</p>
      <p class="dropcap">Born in Glasgow, Scotland, in 1929, Margaret was one of seven children raised amid the poverty and religious divisions of the city’s working-class communities. Her childhood offered little in the way of comfort or certainty. Her father, Hugh Cairns, was in and out of prison and struggled with alcoholism, while her mother, Helen Kennedy, died suddenly when Margaret was just six years old. The Kennedy-Cairns children were left to navigate a precarious existence, relying on one another in circumstances that demanded resourcefulness well beyond their years.</p>
      <p>The Second World War brought further hardship. As German bombs fell on Glasgow, Margaret came of age amid unimaginable adversity. Yet even in those impossible beginnings, the qualities that would define her life were taking shape: an indomitable will, a fierce independence, and a determination to make something more of the hand she’d been dealt.</p>
      <p>Margaret came from a long line of strong, remarkable women. Her maternal grandmother, Helen Orr Kennedy, rose to become a senior leader in Scotland’s women’s Orange Order, a woman of considerable standing in a world far removed from the poverty in which Margaret and her siblings were raised. Margaret’s father largely kept her grandmother away from the children, but Margaret grew up aware of her importance. It was an early glimpse of the influence that women of her generation were so rarely afforded, and of the religious divide that shaped her world. Her grandmother’s Protestant Orange Order stood in marked contrast to her father’s Irish Catholic faith, in which Margaret was raised, a line that ran through Scottish family and community life as both a source of belonging and a boundary between people.</p>
      <p>Raised Catholic, Margaret was studying to become a nun, though the convent, it turned out, could never have held a spirit as restless and bright as hers. At nineteen she made a rather different decision: to cross the Atlantic alone and begin a new life in Montreal, where she would marry a man she barely knew. An ocean now separated her from her brothers and sisters, the people with whom she had survived the hardships of childhood.</p>
      <p>Throughout her twenties, she bore and raised three children within that first marriage, far from the support of her family. Her husband suffered from a brain tumour and was, by every account, a difficult man. Despite her young age, it was a burden she knew well; she had grown up in the shadow of a troubled father, and now, an ocean away, found herself living with one again. These were lonely and difficult beginnings for a young woman in a new country. Making Montreal home would take more than the courage to board a ship; it would take years of finding her footing and building a life of her own.</p>
      <p>Part of that life she found in front of the camera. Through the 1950s Margaret worked as a model, and it was easy to see why: she had striking Scottish looks, with vivid red hair, expressive features, and a smile that could light up a room. She carried that beauty and a youthful radiance well into her later years, along with an instinctive appreciation for elegance and presentation that never left her.</p>
      <p>In the 1960s she married Ted Liontos, a Greek photographer with dashing, James Bond good looks. Their daughter, Anthea, was Margaret’s youngest child. The marriage lasted only a few years, but it gave her more than a daughter. Helping Ted find clients and market his work, Margaret discovered that she had a head for business, and she never quite lost the taste for it.</p>
      <p>Behind the glamour was a woman of real enterprise. Through the 1960s and 1970s, Margaret worked tirelessly to provide for her four children, finding ways to make a living in a world that offered women far fewer paths to financial independence than it does today. She had a talent for connecting with people, a keen eye for opportunity, and a determination that rarely admitted defeat. In another era, she might well have been a formidable entrepreneur. Whatever the limits of her time, she refused to let them set the boundaries of her life.</p>
      <p>Those years brought a change of faith as well. Raised in her father’s Catholic tradition, Margaret discovered Christian Science in the 1970s and became a deeply devoted student of the teachings of Mary Baker Eddy. Her faith was neither casual nor merely ceremonial; she approached it with intellectual curiosity, conviction, and a lifelong desire to understand the relationship between mind, spirit, and the human experience. She took to heart Eddy’s central teaching, set out in “Science and Health”, that God is wholly good and that the spiritual is more real than the material, and it became the lens through which she met both everyday worries and real hardship. In her later years she grew eager to share it. A conversation with Margaret, whether with a grandson, a neighbour, or a nurse she had known for ten minutes, could turn without warning to the power of right thinking, and she made the case with the same conviction and charm she brought to everything else.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="col prose hush">
      <p>Through all of it, her greatest devotion was to her family, and it was there that life would ask the most of her.</p>
      <p>She endured losses that no parent should ever have to bear, including the deaths of her son Colin and daughter Helen, both in their twenties, within thirteen months of one another.</p>
      <p>No amount of strength or determination could spare her that grief. Yet she carried it alongside the hardships of her early life, somehow retaining her appetite for beauty, laughter, conversation, and the company of others.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="col prose">
      <p>For all her independence, Margaret never hesitated to put the needs of those she loved before her own. Her generosity was not merely sentimental; it was practical, instinctive, and often deeply self-sacrificing. When her family needed her, she showed up. When her daughter Anthea was raising a young son on her own while working in Manhattan, Margaret would not hear of leaving him to strangers. She left her beloved Montreal for New York City and helped raise her grandson, Sean, herself.</p>
      <p>For a woman who had guarded her independence so fiercely, it said a great deal that she set it aside so willingly for the people who mattered most. Her love was expressed not only in words or affection, but in the countless ways she made herself indispensable to those around her.</p>
      <p>In later life, her son Paul gave her a small plot of land in her native Scotland, along with a certificate bestowing upon her the honorary title of Lady Margaret. She was touched by the thought but never entirely comfortable with the title, which struck her as faintly fraudulent. The irony was lost on no one else. The girl who had left Glasgow with little to her name had become more of a lady than most people will ever meet, and she had needed no certificate to do it.</p>
      <p>In her later years, as age gradually diminished the independence she had guarded so fiercely, the people who loved her remained close. But those final years were only a small part of a much larger story.</p>
      <p>Those who knew her will remember the woman as much as the story. She was glamorous, opinionated, endlessly sociable, and blessed with a personality that could fill a room before she’d properly entered it. She loved beautiful things, good conversation, and the pleasure of making an impression. Admirers were rarely in short supply; Margaret married four times, and there were plenty of men happy to chase her along the way. She had an eye for style, a gift for storytelling, and an almost supernatural ability to turn an ordinary encounter into an occasion.</p>
      <p>She was quick to laugh, and occasionally quicker to anger, and possessed of a Scottish wit as sharp as her spectacularly stubborn opinions. Her presence was unmistakable, her appetite for life difficult to contain, and life in Margaret’s company was never dull. Whether you knew her for years or met her only briefly, Margaret was someone you remembered.</p>
      <p>She left an impression on nearly everyone she met, and it was rarely a fleeting one. Family, friends, neighbours, and strangers alike found in her a source of wisdom, love, warmth, and kindness, given freely and often just when it was needed. People tended to leave her company a little wiser, a little more cared for, and usually with a story to tell.</p>
      <p>Music was another of Margaret’s great gifts. Blessed with a beautiful singing voice, she carried on a musical tradition that ran through her family, which also included her niece, the celebrated Scottish singer Lulu. Margaret was rarely far from a song, and her voice filled the family home with music, warmth, and life. She was still singing in her very last days, and for those who knew her, the sound of Margaret’s voice will be among the most enduring memories of all.</p>
      <p>Above all, Margaret loved her family. Her affection was fierce, her loyalty unwavering, and her generosity boundless. Those closest to her knew a woman of wonderful contradictions: tender and tempestuous, elegant and irreverent, independent and deeply attached. She could be demanding, exasperating, and gloriously entertaining, often all in the same afternoon. But no one who belonged to Margaret’s family ever had reason to doubt the depth of her love.</p>
      <p>Her life carried her from the poverty of wartime Glasgow to the elegance of mid-century Montreal, through extraordinary social transformations, personal reinventions, heartbreaks, and triumphs. Along the way she had been a model, a clerk, an entrepreneur, a socialite and a grande dame, whatever she needed to be to survive and to care for those she loved. She was also a mother, a grandmother, a woman of faith, and a force of nature.</p>
      <p>She was predeceased by her beloved children, Colin and Helen; her longtime partner, George Kendall; and her siblings, including her sister Nellie and brother James. She is survived by her son, Paul, her daughter, Anthea, and her grandson, Sean, who will carry forward her memory with enormous love, admiration, laughter, and gratitude.</p>
      <p>In accordance with her wishes, Margaret will be cremated, and her ashes scattered on Mount Royal, where she will join Colin and Helen once more.</p>
      <p>Even in her final days, her wit was as sharp as ever. When nurses explained that they needed to take medical images of her ribs, the former model asked, without missing a beat, “Am I being paid for these pictures?” Some things, it seems, never change.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col prose to-book">
      <p><a href="/memories">Share a memory of Margaret, or read what others have written, in the memory book.</a></p>
    </div>

    {CHARITY.name && (
      <section class="col prose tribute">
        <p>In lieu of flowers, the family asks that you consider a donation to {CHARITY.name}, a cause close to Margaret’s heart.</p>
        <a class="btn" href={CHARITY.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donate to {CHARITY.name}</a>
      </section>
    )}
  </main>

  <footer class="col">
    <p>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · Glasgow 1929 · Montreal 2026</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/motion.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
